<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rusyns</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carpatho-Rusyns</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rusyns are an East Slavic people of the Carpathian mountains, living mainly in Transcarpathian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with a large diaspora in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Most belong to the Greek Catholic or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches. Their homeland formed the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Subcarpathian Ruthenia</a>, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> between the world wars, and they are recognised as a national minority in several countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rusyns.jpg" alt="Rusyns" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: YoungstownToast · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rusyns_2007.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Rusyn comes from the archaic ethnonym Rus', and the people have also been called Carpatho-Rusyns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a> and Rusnaks. They speak Rusyn, an East Slavic language variety treated either as a distinct language or as a dialect of Ukrainian. Their homeland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Carpathian Ruthenia</a>, is a cross-border region that takes in Subcarpathian Rus', the Prešov Region in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, the Lemko Region in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Maramureş in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><p>There are different theories of their origin. Paul Robert Magocsi holds that it is complex and not exclusively related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. Their ancestors included early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> whose movement to the Danubian Basin was influenced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and Pannonian Avars between the 5th and 6th centuries, the White Croats who lived on both slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, the Rusyns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>, and Vlach shepherds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Ukrainian encyclopedias generally consider them descendants of the White Croats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Austro-Hungarian monarchy controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> from 1772 to 1918. In the 19th century increasing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyarisation">Magyarization</a> led some educated Rusyns to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, while other intellectuals favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. A Rusyn national movement formed that stressed a distinct ethnic identity and literary language. On 2 May 1848, during the Spring of Nations, the Main Ruthenian Council was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lemberg</a> as the first political representation of the Galician Rusyns, and Greek-Catholic clergy were the most active stratum among them.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> the term Ruthene was the official name for the Rusyns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Transcarpathia</a> until 1945. According to Magocsi, after 1945 Communist authorities treated Carpatho-Rusyns as a branch of the Ukrainian nationality and Rusyn as a dialect of Ukrainian, and banned Rusyn nationality and language. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a> there has been a revival of Rusyn culture and identity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Many Rusyns are Eastern Catholics of the Byzantine Rite who have been in communion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> since the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzhhorod">Uzhhorod</a> in 1646. Their church, the Ruthenian Greek Catholic Church, keeps the Byzantine liturgy, sometimes in Church Slavonic, and married priests. Orthodoxy also has a place: in the 1890s a movement began to return to it, and Alexis Toth, a former Greek Catholic priest, led as many as 20,000 Rusyn Americans to Orthodoxy.</p><p>Rusyn communities also settled outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>. The oldest diaspora community lies in the Pannonian Plain, in parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. At the end of the 19th and the start of the 20th century about 225,000 Rusyns emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, mostly to the industrial centres of the Northeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rusyns” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1921 census counted about 13.6 million people, of whom Czechs and Slovaks, officially treated as one Czechoslovak nation, formed about two-thirds; there were also more than three million Germans, about 750,000 Hungarians, <b>Ruthenes</b>, Jews and Poles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leaders hoped for union with Russia or with the <b>Rusyns</b> of Czechoslovakia and sent delegates to the Paris Peace Conference, without success.</blockquote><a href="/state/lemko-rusyn-republic">Lemko-Rusyn Republic</a></li></ul></section>
